Project 2: The Price Tracker (Logic & Blueprint)
This project relies on requesting, parsing, and scheduling.
You are requesting a document from a server, searching it for a specific piece of text, and deciding if that text meets a criteria.

🧠 The Logic Flow
Fetch the Webpage: Send an HTTP "GET" request to the product URL, mimicking a real web browser.

Parse the HTML: Convert the massive wall of raw HTML text into a structured tree that Python can easily search.

Locate the Price: Find the exact HTML element (usually an id or a class) where the price is stored and extract the raw text (e.g., "$1,249.00").

Clean the Data: Convert that text into a raw number. Python cannot mathematically compare "$1,249.00" to 1000.
You must strip away the $, commas, and whitespace, then convert it to a float (1249.00).

Evaluate & Alert: Compare the current price to your target price. If it is lower, trigger an action (like printing an alert, sending an email, or sending a text).

📋 Action Steps to Take
Step 1: Open the product page in your web browser, right-click the price, and click Inspect.
Look closely at the HTML tag. Does it have a unique id (like id="priceblock_ourprice") or a generic class? You need this identifier for your script.

Step 2: Plan for webpage structure changes. Websites change their design constantly.
If they change their price tag class, your script will break. You must design your script to fail gracefully (e.g., "Could not find price element") instead of crashing.


